        "prompt": "Research what a Twenty application (twenty-sdk app format) can do regarding standard objects and permissions, in this repo. I need a factual report with exact file paths. Research only; do not modify anything.\n\nContext: apps live under packages/twenty-apps/internal/ (e.g. a2e-projects, a2e-accounting, real-estate). The app manifest/config is an `application.config.ts` and app objects/fields are declared in the app source.\n\nFind and report:\n1. The application manifest/config format: `defineApplication` or `ApplicationConfig` type. Which top-level keys exist (objects, fields, frontComponents, commandMenuItems, pageLayouts, logicFunctions, roles, permissions, navigationMenuItems, searchProviders, etc.)? Look in packages/twenty-sdk/src/ and packages/twenty-apps/internal/real-estate/.\n2. Can an app extend a STANDARD object (not app-owned)? Specifically, can an app add a field to a standard object like `calendarEvent` or `task`, or add a relation from a standard object to an app object? Look for examples — does any internal app add fields to standard objects? Search packages/twenty-apps/internal/**/src for object definitions targeting standard objects.\n3. Application fields/permissions: how does an app declare permissions/roles? Look for `roles`, `permissions`, `defineRole` in the SDK and internal apps. Is there a way to declare which standard objects a role can access?\n4. App activation/installation: what happens on install (objects, roles, views, nav). Is there any concept of app \"activation boundary\" (enabled/disabled state, feature flags, per-workspace app settings)? Look for `application` entity fields (e.g. isActive, status, universalIdentifier) under packages/twenty-server/src/engine/core-modules/application/.\n5. Can an app own/define a standard-like object named `calendarEvent`? Is that allowed or reserved? (i.e., can an app shadow a standard object — presumably no.) Search for reserved names / standard object collision checks.\n\nReport exact paths and key findings. Be thorough (\"very thorough\"). Return factual findings with file paths. Do not modify anything.",
